          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.107,
            0.885,
            0.162
          ],
          "content": "• The formula for the length of a quarter circle may also be written as $2 \\pi r \\times \\frac { 9 0 ^ { \\circ } } { 3 6 0 ^ { \\circ } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.178,
            0.886,
            0.238
          ],
          "content": "From these, we can guess the formula for the length of an arc of a circle in terms of the angle it makes (i.e., the angle it ‘subtends’) at the centre of the circle."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.265,
            0.387,
            0.402
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.168,
                0.408,
                0.399,
                0.443
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.10: Length of an arc of a circle"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.451,
            0.275,
            0.879,
            0.357
          ],
          "content": "If the arc is AB, and it subtends an angle $\\theta ^ { \\circ }$ at the centre O of the circle, the length of the arc is $2 \\pi r \\times \\frac { \\theta ^ { \\circ } } { 3 6 0 ^ { \\circ } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
